<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edwin Barclay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edwin Barclay was president of Liberia from 1930 to 1944. Taking office after the forced-labour scandal that brought down President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-d-b-king">Charles D. B. King</a>, he resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> proposals for international control of Liberia, renegotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firestone-tire-and-rubber-company">Firestone</a> loan, and brought Liberia into close alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edwin-barclay.jpg" alt="Edwin Barclay" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edwin_Barclay_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edwin James Barclay was a politician, poet and musician. His paternal grandparents moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> to Liberia with their children in 1865, part of a minority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> immigrants who shared with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americo-liberians">Americo-Liberians</a> a culture with an English base, considerable mixed-race ancestry and a common history. His father, Ernest Barclay, and his uncle Arthur Barclay became important Liberian politicians. At 19, in 1901, Edwin wrote a patriotic song, &quot;The Lone Star Forever&quot;. A member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-whig-party">True Whig Party</a>, he served as secretary of state in the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-d-b-king">Charles D. B. King</a> from 1920 to 1930.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Barclay became president in 1930, when King and Vice President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allen-yancy">Allen Yancy</a> resigned after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> investigation implicated them in profiting from forced labour. He completed King's term and was first elected in his own right in 1931. One of his first decisions was to repeal the Port of Entry Law of 1864, which had restricted the economic activities of foreigners, and in the early 1930s concessions were signed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, Danish, German and Polish investors.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> collapsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> prices and Liberia's revenues. In 1926 the government had granted a rubber concession to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firestone-tire-and-rubber-company">Firestone Tire and Rubber Company</a> and obtained a $5 million loan through a Firestone subsidiary. By 1931 Liberia could not meet its payments, and in 1932 the legislature declared a moratorium pending renegotiation. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> commission recommended financial help with foreign advisers in key posts; some members of the League advocated a form of international control, which the Liberian government resisted. A reorganisation plan under League supervision was put into effect in 1934, and Liberia kept its formal independence.</p><p>In 1937, under American pressure, Barclay withdrew the concession given to the German investors, who were accused of Nazi sympathies. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>-producing areas of the Far East, Liberia became one of the few sources of natural rubber left to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and Barclay promised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> all it needed. In 1942 Liberia signed a defence pact with the United States, which brought roads, airports and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roberts-international-airport">Roberts Field</a>, a staging point on the South Atlantic air ferry route. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> visited Liberia in January 1943, and Barclay addressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> in May 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Barclay retired on 3 January 1944 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tubman">William Tubman</a>. He stood against Tubman in the presidential election of 1955 and received only about 1,100 votes. He died on 6 November 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edwin Barclay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Edwin Barclay</b> (1930–1944) resisted proposals for international trusteeship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/liberia">Liberia</a>. Died in Liberia. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.31,-10.81&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Monrovia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edwin Barclay"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4087 4461h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4087 4461h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.31,-10.81&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
